 red territory and Trump/Braun disapproval in Indiana", "Open-seat uncertainty in a presidential race with a wide historical range"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican lean of about nine points, and with no polls or ratings available there is little basis to move far from that. Recent coverage of Democratic strength in red-state races and Indiana disapproval of Trump and Braun justifies only a modest shift toward Democrats, keeping Indiana likely Republican.", "watch": "Post-midterm polling of the 2028 field in Indiana, and whether Democratic red-state momentum persists into the presidential cycle, could narrow or widen this margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Indiana remains a Republican-leaning state in presidential elections, and the 2028 race is an open contest with no incumbent on the ballot. The partisan baseline gives Republicans an edge of roughly nine points, and no state-level general-election polls or expert ratings have yet been published to refine that picture.\n\nRecent coverage offers some reason for Democratic optimism: polls show Hoosiers disapproving of Trump and Governor Braun, and Democrats have posted strong showings in Republican-leaning governor races this fall. Still, a presidential race in a state this red historically favors the GOP, so the forecast keeps Indiana in the likely Republican column with a margin near seven points and wide uncertainty given the thin evidence base.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1505, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2049, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2014, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2053, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2035, "margin": -9.0}, {"d